selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"../exit_cone/ExitCone.html#pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.ExitCone\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.ExitCone\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.exit_cone.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ExitCone</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../port/Port.html#pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port\"><code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.port.Port</span></code></a></p><p>How a volute\u2019s outlet duct leaves, and what it grows into.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Exit-cone geometry reaches the loss analysis three ways.\n<code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">final_diameter</span></code> sets station 7 and, with the arc length, the angle\nthe cone opens out at \u2013 which is most of what decides its diffusion loss.\nThe arc length also sets the wall area friction is charged over, and the\nturn sets the bend loss.</p><p>That makes <code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">arc_length_parameter</span></code> a real design variable rather than\na drawing detail: a longer duct opens out more gradually and recovers more\nof the velocity head, at the cost of more wetted wall and, for a given\nturn, a gentler bend. <code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">loss_breakdown()</span></code>\nreports the three apart so the trade can be seen.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._resolve_exit\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._resolve_exit\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">_resolve_exit</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_cone</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.solve\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">solve</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet_state</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\" title=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">mass_flow</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">transport</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_diameter</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">|</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/constants.html#None\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">roughness</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/functions.html#float\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">float</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">friction_model</span></span><span class=\"p\"><span class=\"pre\">:</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\"><a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://docs.python.org/3/builtins/stdtypes.html#str\" title=\"(in Python v3.15)\"><span class=\"pre\">str</span></a></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">'fluids'</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span> <span class=\"sig-return\"><span class=\"sig-return-icon\">\u2192</span> <span class=\"sig-return-typehint\"><a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteResult</span></a></span></span></dt><dd><p>Run Aungier\u2019s meanline loss analysis at one operating point.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._solve_size\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._solve_size\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">_solve_size</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">theta</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>The smallest section satisfying the area law at <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">theta</span></code>.</p><p>The three regimes are the same ones a manifold works through, so the\ncascade itself is shared; what a volute supplies is its own residual.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._resolve\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._resolve\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">_resolve</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_stations</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.outlet_center\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.outlet_center\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">outlet_center</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Centre of the volute outlet bore, in machine coordinates [m].</p><p>This is the point an exit cone leaves from, and the axis its flange is\ncarried along.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../area_law/ConstantAngularMomentum.html#pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantAngularMomentum\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantAngularMomentum\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ConstantAngularMomentum</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AreaLaw</span></code></p><p>Size the passage to conserve the angular momentum it collects.</p><p>Imposing <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(r*C_u)_c</span> <span class=\"pre\">=</span> <span class=\"pre\">(r*C_u)_5</span></code> together with mass conservation gives\nAungier\u2019s Eqs. 11-1 to 11-3, generalised by the sizing parameter in\nEq. 11-5:</p><p>so the area divided by the centroid radius grows linearly with azimuth.\nBecause both <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">A</span></code> and <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">r_c</span></code> follow from the section size, this is\nimplicit in the size and is solved rather than evaluated.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._residual\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._residual\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">_residual</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">size</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">theta</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">aspect</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Area-law miss for one trial section.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"VoluteResult.html#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.VoluteResult\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">VoluteResult</span></span></dt><dd><p>Loss breakdown and station conditions for one volute operating point.</p><p>All loss coefficients are referred to the inlet velocity head, so a total\npressure loss follows as <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">omega</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">rho</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">C5**2</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">2</span></code>.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_points</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">64</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Section boundaries swept into machine coordinates.</p><p>The machine axis is <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">z</span></code> and the tongue sits at azimuth zero. Each\nstation\u2019s section is placed in the plane through the axis at its\nazimuth, rotated about the local tangential direction by\n<code class=\"xref py py-attr docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">tilt</span></code>, with the slot mouth centred on the inlet radius.\nAzimuth advances toward <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">+y</span></code> for a counterclockwise volute and\ntoward <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">-y</span></code> for a clockwise one.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../../common/friction/index.html#pyskyfire.common.friction.darcy_friction_factor\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.common.friction.darcy_friction_factor\">\n<span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.common.friction.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">darcy_friction_factor</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">ReDh</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">Dh</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">roughness</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">model</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">'native'</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">laminar_factor</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">1.0</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Return the Darcy friction factor for a straight duct.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">Volute</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">section</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">inlet</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">area_law</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"keyword-only-separator o\"><abbr title=\"Keyword-only parameters separator (PEP 3102)\"><span class=\"pre\">*</span></abbr></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">rotation</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">exit_cone</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">None</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">tilt</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">0.0</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_stations</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">181</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>A volute or collector resolved from a section family and an area law.</p><p class=\"rubric\">Notes</p><p>Geometry is resolved on construction and is independent of the operating\npoint, so one volute can be solved at several mass flows.</p><p>Tilt rotates the section plane rigidly, and rotation mirrors the whole\npassage about a plane through the axis, so both change where the passage\nsits in space but leave every scalar in the loss analysis unchanged. They\nmatter for packaging and for <a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep()</span></code></a>, not for pressure loss.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../../thermo/state/FluidState.html#pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.thermo.state.FluidState\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.thermo.state.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">FluidState</span></span></dt><dd><p>Resolved bulk fluid state at one axial station.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"../area_law/ConstantArea.html#pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantArea\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.ConstantArea\">\n<span class=\"property\"><span class=\"k\"><span class=\"pre\">class</span></span><span class=\"w\"> </span></span><span class=\"sig-prename descclassname\"><span class=\"pre\">pyskyfire.ducts.area_law.</span></span><span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">ConstantArea</span></span></dt><dd><p>Bases: <code class=\"xref py py-obj docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">AreaLaw</span></code></p><p>Hold the passage area uniform all the way around.</p><p>This is Aungier\u2019s \u201ccollector\u201d rather than a volute. It collects the flow\nwithout conserving angular momentum, so its sizing parameter is whatever\nthe chosen area happens to produce and its tangential-velocity loss is\ncorrespondingly larger. Aungier\u2019s Eq. 5-108 additionally averages that loss\nagainst a total loss of tangential head, because a uniform passage tries to\ndiffuse the entering flow to rest where collection begins.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep_exit_cone\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep_exit_cone\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep_exit_cone</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_points</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">64</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">n_stations</span></span><span class=\"o\"><span class=\"pre\">=</span></span><span class=\"default_value\"><span class=\"pre\">17</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>The exit-cone duct swept into machine coordinates.</p><p>The duct starts as the volute\u2019s own outlet section, sampled exactly as\n<a class=\"reference internal\" href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\" title=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute.sweep\"><code class=\"xref py py-meth docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">sweep()</span></code></a> samples it \u2013 the same call, at the same size, with the\nsame <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">n_points</span></code> \u2013 so its first ring is bit-identical to the\nvolute\u2019s own last one rather than merely tracing the same curve at\ndifferent points along it. That identity is what makes the two sweeps\nstitch into one seamless duct wall.</p><p>Each of those vertices then keeps its own angle about the outlet\ncentre fixed for the whole duct, while its radius blends by a\nsmoothstep toward the flange radius, so the wall leaves the volute and\nmeets the flange without a crease. The frame it blends within starts as\nthe volute\u2019s own tilted section-plane basis, not the untilted\nmachine-frame default, so a tilted volute\u2019s cutwater edge continues to\nrotate smoothly into the flange instead of snapping to meet it.</p></dd>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire-ducts-volute-volute\"]": "<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#pyskyfire-ducts-volute-volute\" title=\"Link to this heading\">\u00b6</a></h1>", "a[href=\"#pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._exit_cone_losses\"]": "<dt class=\"sig sig-object py\" id=\"pyskyfire.ducts.volute.Volute._exit_cone_losses\">\n<span class=\"sig-name descname\"><span class=\"pre\">_exit_cone_losses</span></span><span class=\"sig-paren\">(</span><em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">rho</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">mu</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">C_5</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">C_6</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">C_7</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">roughness</span></span></em>, <em class=\"sig-param\"><span class=\"n\"><span class=\"pre\">friction_model</span></span></em><span class=\"sig-paren\">)</span></dt><dd><p>Diffusion, wall friction and bend loss of the exit-cone duct.</p><p>All three are referred to the inlet velocity head, like every other\ncoefficient here.</p><p>An exit cone is a conical transition, and how gradually it opens out\nor closes in is most of what decides its loss. Aungier\u2019s Eq. 5-110\ncharges the whole velocity head change, <code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">(C_6</span> <span class=\"pre\">-</span> <span class=\"pre\">C_7)**2</span></code>, which is\nthe Borda-Carnot loss of a <em>sudden</em> expansion and carries no angle\ndependence at all: it returns the same loss for a duct that opens out\nover three degrees as for one that dumps straight into the pipe. That\nis a fair model only when there is no cone to speak of, so it is kept\nfor exactly that case, and the matching Rennels correlation is used\nwhenever there is a real cone to resolve. The diffuser branch\napproaches the same Borda-Carnot value as the included angle opens\ntoward 180 degrees, so the two agree where they should.</p><p>A flange narrower than the passage feeding it is a contraction rather\nthan a diffuser, and a much cheaper one \u2013 Borda-Carnot is not merely\nangle-blind there but the wrong shape of formula, since it is derived\nfor a flow that separates on expanding. Both directions take their own\nRennels correlation, which is why the coefficient is referred to\nwhichever end is the faster: the passage for a diffuser, the flange\nfor a contraction.</p><p>Rennels carries its own wall-friction term either way, so friction is\nnot charged again separately. Evaluating the correlation twice, once\nat the real friction factor and once at zero, splits its coefficient\ninto the transition and friction parts the breakdown reports. As the\ncone flattens toward a plain tube that friction term tends to\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">darcy</span> <span class=\"pre\">*</span> <span class=\"pre\">length</span> <span class=\"pre\">/</span> <span class=\"pre\">diameter</span></code>, which is what the untapered branch\ncharges, so the two meet without a step. The bend uses the same\n<code class=\"docutils literal notranslate\"><span class=\"pre\">fluids</span></code> correlation the pipe solver uses for a rounded bend, at the\nduct\u2019s own centreline radius and turn.</p></dd>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(` ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 600, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
